y1-life.content:
This course (together with Life Imagined A) provides an overview of developments in the arts (from their earliest verbal, visual and performative expressions to the recent emergence of new media and the waning of the traditional boundaries between art genres) and in those branches of the humanities that study the arts. The arts and humanities are discussed in their relations to historical events, social changes and transformations in world views. Rather than approaching the individual arts in isolation (film, theater, literature, visual arts, music), we will study them in their interconnection. The focus will be on the relation between the arts and:
- the emergence of and developments in the humanities;
- changes in culture, and historical context;
- technological innovations and other changes that affect the media in which experience is expressed, such as the human body, material objects (sculptures, buildings), spoken and written language (the voice, the book), analogue and digital means for recording and storing material (vinyl, DVD, the internet), etc.
This course does not pretend to cover all areas and all traditions in the rich and complex history of the arts. Instead, we will focus on a number of paradigmatic cultural transformations that have affected and shaped the (mainly Western-European) tradition. In this second half of the course we will critically reflect on the dominant Western tradition that we discussed in the first part. While in the first half, we see ‘humankind’ and the ‘autonomous individual’ take center stage, the second half will discuss several developments, both scholarly and societal, that decentered the ‘human’.
